&lt;td style="text-align: right"&gt;204 MiB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td style="text-align: right"&gt;1,370 MiB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;gaia, 20 slides&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td style="text-align: right"&gt;112 ms&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td style="text-align: right"&gt;4,608 ms&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td style="text-align: right"&gt;893 KiB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td style="text-align: right"&gt;4.8 MiB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td style="text-align: right"&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td style="text-align: right"&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;math and code, 20 slides&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td style="text-align: right"&gt;207 ms&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td style="text-align: right"&gt;2,780 ms&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td style="text-align: right"&gt;842 KiB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td style="text-align: right"&gt;1.5 MiB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td style="text-align: right"&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td style="text-align: right"&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;
&lt;p&gt;For the 100-slide deck, v0.4.0 took 4.7 s, 4.8 MiB and 540 MiB. marp-cli&amp;rsquo;s
PPTX holds pictures. Most of an editable deck&amp;rsquo;s size is the embedded fonts.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 data-hextra-search-id="why-it-is-fast"&gt;Why it is fast&lt;span class="hx:absolute hx:-mt-20" id="why-it-is-fast"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;a href="#why-it-is-fast" class="subheading-anchor" aria-label="Permalink for this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;No browser to start.&lt;/strong&gt; marp-cli launches Chrome for every PDF, image and
PPTX export; go-slides renders with
&lt;a href="https://github.com/go-webengine/engine" target="_blank" rel="noopener"&gt;go-webengine&lt;/a&gt; inside its own
process.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Slides render in parallel&lt;/strong&gt;, four at a time by default, and each image is
written as soon as it is its turn: a long deck is never held in memory as
images.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Fonts are parsed once&lt;/strong&gt; per process, not once per slide.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h2 data-hextra-search-id="what-it-does-not-win"&gt;What it does not win&lt;span class="hx:absolute hx:-mt-20" id="what-it-does-not-win"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;a href="#what-it-does-not-win" class="subheading-anchor" aria-label="Permalink for this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;PDF size, in v0.3.0.&lt;/strong&gt; Each slide was an image in the PDF: 10 MiB against
837 KiB for the 100-slide deck. The vector PDF above is smaller than
marp-cli&amp;rsquo;s.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;CPU time on big decks.&lt;/strong&gt; Four workers spend more CPU than the wall time
shows: in v0.3.0, 3.2 s against marp-cli&amp;rsquo;s 2.7 s for the 100-slide PDF
(0.9 s for the vector PDF).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Memory is an upper bound for marp-cli&lt;/strong&gt;: Chrome&amp;rsquo;s processes share pages
that the sum counts in each. Its largest single process is 220–320 MiB.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The method, every deck and mode, and the commands to reproduce the figures
are in
&lt;a href="https://github.com/go-slides/slides/blob/main/BENCHMARKS.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener"&gt;BENCHMARKS.md&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item></channel></rss>